# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored.
#
# Every name below is one the code actually reads — checked, not assumed. The
# previous version of this file had drifted: it asked for `WARDEN_PRIVATE_KEY`
# and `MISQUOTE_POSITION_CAP_USD`, which nothing reads, while omitting
# `MISQUOTE_PRIVATE_KEY` and `MISQUOTE_POSITION_CAP_QUOTE`, which the signer and
# the go/no-go do. Filling that version in correctly still produced a config
# that did nothing, silently.
#
# Nothing here is required to run `make test`, `make advantage-demo`, `make vet`,
# `make warden` or `make indexer-follow`. All of those work on public endpoints
# today. What these unlock is history, and eventually signing.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. A KEYED MAINNET RPC — nice to have, and no longer required
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This file used to call this "THE ONE THAT MATTERS" and say the 30-day tape was
# blocked on it. That was wrong, and the cause was ours rather than the
# endpoints'.
#
# Surveyed 16 Aug 2026, one request at a time: of 22 public BSC endpoints, eight
# — every bnbchain dataseed, both defibit hosts, ninicoin — answer `eth_chainId`
# with 56 in milliseconds and refuse **every** `eth_getLogs` with `-32005`,
# including a one-block query over an address holding no logs. Two of the three
# endpoints this project had configured were in that group, and the health check
# selected on chain id, which all of them pass. So the indexer was choosing nodes
# that could never serve it, and a tail refused on every request looks exactly
# like a tail working on a pool nobody trades. See requirements-matrix P-11.
#
# Three endpoints do serve logs, capped at 5,000 blocks a request. Measured:
# **40 consecutive back-to-back requests, zero refusals, median latency 12.0s.**
# A 30-day tape is 1,152 requests — about an hour, on free endpoints, with this
# left blank. `uv run python -m misquote.indexer.backfill --days 30`.
#
# What a key still buys: speed, one endpoint that will not disappear, and a
# margin if the three that work stop working. Free tiers that are enough:
# NodeReal MegaNode, QuickNode, Ankr, Chainstack, BlockPI, dRPC. It needs no
# funds and no wallet — it is a read key.
BSC_RPC_URL=

# Chapel (97). Only needed for a testnet burn-in.
BSC_TESTNET_RPC_URL=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. ARCHIVE RPC — optional, improves reproducibility
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `make fork-diff` compares our position math against the real
# NonfungiblePositionManager on an anvil fork. With an archive endpoint the fork
# pins a block and the comparison is reproducible; without one it falls back to
# near-head and says so in the output. No free BSC endpoint serves archive state.
BSC_ARCHIVE_RPC_URL=

# What anvil forks from. Defaults to BSC_ARCHIVE_RPC_URL, then BSC_RPC_URL.
BSC_FORK_RPC_URL=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. WALLET — testnet only, and not yet useful
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read this before generating anything: **there is no chain executor in this
# repository.** `agents/warden/live.py` defines the `Executor` protocol and one
# implementation, `SimulatedExecutor`, which moves a position in memory. So a
# funded wallet cannot yet cause a transaction, because the code that would
# build one has not been written. It is on the not-built ledger.
#
# When it exists it will be built and tested against an anvil fork first, using
# anvil's own funded accounts — which needs nothing from you.
#
# So supply this only when a real testnet burn-in is the next step.
#
#   - A DEDICATED wallet, generated for this and used for nothing else.
#   - Never a wallet holding anything you would mind losing entirely.
#   - Testnet BNB from https://testnet.bnbchain.org/faucet-smart
#
# How long to wait for a receipt before giving up, in seconds. Default 180.
# A timeout here is usually the RPC, not the chain: two mainnet runs have
# died in this wait after their transactions had already mined. Read chain
# state before resending — a timeout is not a failure.
MISQUOTE_RECEIPT_TIMEOUT_S=

# The signer refuses to broadcast unless MISQUOTE_DRY_RUN is explicitly "0".
MISQUOTE_PRIVATE_KEY=

# The wallet this deployment declares as its own, and the only thing standing
# between a mistyped key and a transaction from the wrong address.
#
# Set this and two things start checking it. `make go-no-go --mainnet` derives
# the address from MISQUOTE_PRIVATE_KEY and FAILS when it is not this one —
# replacing the gate's old last instruction, which was "verify by hand that it
# is not a main wallet". And `chain/signer.py` refuses to construct at all when
# broadcasting is enabled and the key signs for somebody else.
#
# Leave it blank and both fall back to what they did before: the gate reports
# amber and says nothing checked which wallet the key is. A *malformed* value is
# not the same as a blank one and is rejected rather than ignored — setting this
# is an act that tightens a check, and a typo must not be able to turn it off.
#
# An ordinary address, checksummed or lowercase. Never a key.
MISQUOTE_OPERATOR_ADDRESS=

# The wallet that actually holds the key above, when it is not the operator's.
#
# Set this and the key is checked against *it* instead. That is not a loophole:
# a key matching neither declaration is refused exactly as before, and the
# go/no-go reports a green gate only when the operator signs for itself — when
# the two differ it goes amber and says "signing as a delegate of", because a
# nominated wallet spending is a weaker claim than the operator spending.
#
# It exists because the two are genuinely different jobs. The operator is an
# identity that receives ERC-8004 registrations and would be judged; its key
# belongs somewhere other than a laptop running `make`. The signer is a hot
# wallet holding faucet funds, and its key has to be here because that is the
# point of it. `scripts/register_identity.py` uses exactly this shape.
#
# Leave blank for the ordinary single-wallet case.
MISQUOTE_SIGNER_ADDRESS=

# The operator's own key — only when the operator itself has to sign.
#
# Read the trade-off before setting this. The two-wallet split above exists
# precisely so a submission identity's key does not have to live on a machine
# that runs `make`; setting this puts it there, and `.env` being gitignored is
# the only thing standing between it and a public repository.
#
# What it buys is the ability to *correct what the operator owns*. The ERC-8004
# registry's `setAgentURI` is owner-only, so a card that landed incomplete can
# only be rewritten by the wallet holding the token — and after
# `scripts/register_identity.py` transfers an identity, that is the operator and
# nobody else. Without this, a registration is final at the moment it is handed
# over.
#
# Unset is the safe default and costs only that. Everything else keeps working
# from MISQUOTE_PRIVATE_KEY, signing as the declared delegate.
MISQUOTE_OPERATOR_PRIVATE_KEY=

# The most this will pay for gas, in wei per unit. Unset means 1 gwei.
#
# There was no ceiling until a mainnet run on a 0.0057 BNB wallet went looking
# for one: `BscSigner.build` takes `w3.eth.gas_price` verbatim and multiplies
# the estimate by 1.25, so the only thing between a price spike and an empty
# wallet was the balance — a limit that announces itself by the *last*
# transaction failing, after the earlier ones are paid for.
#
# 1 gwei is twenty times BSC's typical 0.05. A refusal, not a clamp: bidding
# under the node's price produces a transaction that sits unmined, which is a
# worse failure than being told the price moved. Set 0 to disable deliberately.
MISQUOTE_MAX_GAS_PRICE_WEI=

# How many processes a replay forks. Unset = the cgroup quota, then affinity.
#
# `os.cpu_count()` reports the *host's* cores, not this container's share, and
# each fork copies the event list — refcounting writes to every object, so
# copy-on-write does not help. On a small instance that gets the container
# killed rather than merely slowed, and the queue is ephemeral, so the job
# vanishes mid-run with nothing to read afterwards.
MISQUOTE_REPLAY_JOBS=

# 0 = the API serves and nothing drains the queue. Unset (or 1) = a worker runs.
#
# Honoured by two places that between them cover every way this gets deployed:
# `scripts/serve.sh`, which starts the worker beside uvicorn, and the API's own
# startup hook, which starts one when nothing else has. The second exists
# because on the instance this actually runs on the start command is *not*
# serve.sh — `/worker` reported no pid and no log in a directory the API writes
# `jobs.db` into — so every hire queued behind a worker nobody started.
#
# One variable governs both, so switching the worker off does not depend on
# knowing which of the two started it.
MISQUOTE_API_WORKER=

# Where the API's worker writes its output, and where its pid is recorded.
#
# `scripts/serve.sh` backgrounds the worker with `&`, which detaches it from
# `set -eu`: a worker that dies at startup takes the queue with it and says
# nothing. The API keeps accepting jobs, `workers_alive` reads 0, and
# `worker_last_seen` reads null — the same two readings a worker that was never
# started produces, which is a different bug.
#
# `GET /worker` serves the tail of this file, so on a host whose logs are not to
# hand the answer arrives over HTTP. Unset is fine; both default under `data/`.
MISQUOTE_WORKER_LOG=
MISQUOTE_WORKER_PID=

# 1 (or unset) = read the chain, never sign. The default, and a missing config
# therefore refuses to trade rather than trading. Setting this to 0 is a
# deliberate act and the go/no-go reports it.
MISQUOTE_DRY_RUN=1

# The most capital a position may hold, in the pool's token1. The go/no-go FAILS
# when this is unset under --mainnet: the plan says it is chosen at that gate,
# deliberately, and never defaulted.
MISQUOTE_POSITION_CAP_QUOTE=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4. OPTIONAL
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Toxicity's CEX leg. Unset means the on-chain fallback of spec section 3.4
# applies, and every card says which rule it used. The live run so far has been
# on the fallback: "the CEX price feed was unavailable on 99% of samples".
CEX_FEED_URL=wss://stream.binance.com:9443/ws/bnbusdt@bookTicker

DB_PATH=./data/misquote.db
MISQUOTE_JOURNAL_DIR=./data/journal

# Where the API reads the artifacts from. Unset is the normal case: `service.py`
# resolves them relative to its own `__file__`, which lands inside the checkout
# so long as the install is editable. Set it when it is not — a wheel installed
# into site-packages resolves to site-packages, and the service then comes up
# healthy serving zero artifacts, because `/health` is deliberately about the
# process rather than the data. See `render.yaml`, which chooses `-e` over
# setting this precisely because a hardcoded absolute host path is the more
# brittle of the two.
MISQUOTE_ARTIFACTS=

# Where the job store lives. Separate from DB_PATH on purpose: the tape is
# read-mostly evidence that gets rebuilt, and rebuilding it should not erase the
# record of what was queued. `indexer/store.connect()` also executes its schema
# on every call, so job tables kept there would appear in every tmp_path
# database a test opens. Unset is the normal case.
MISQUOTE_JOBS_DB=

# Where the client looks for the live API, published into
# `apps/web/public/artifacts/api.json` by `make api-config`. Unset publishes
# `base: null`, which is the honest default — every page renders from the
# artifacts alone, and a client that finds null must not fall back to the site
# origin, because there is no API there.
MISQUOTE_API_BASE=

# Pool and chain. Both default to the verified target pool; override to point the
# indexer or the badge somewhere else.
TARGET_POOL=
TARGET_CHAIN_ID=56

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5. 8004scan — free, and it changes what the registry artifact may claim
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 8004scan.io indexes the same ERC-8004 registries we read on chain. Without a
# key its public API answers 180 requests a minute and 20,000 a day; with one,
# 3,000 a minute and 3,000,000 a day. Those four numbers come off the
# `x-ratelimit-limit-*` response headers. The published docs say 600 and 100,000
# for the keyed tier and are wrong.
#
# What the key buys is NOT speed. The whole-chain census is 2,848 pages and 60
# to 130 minutes with a key or without one, because 8004scan's /agents slows
# with offset depth rather than with our request rate — raising concurrency to
# spend the new headroom made it measurably worse.
#
# What it buys is that the index answers directly. `x402_supported=true` returns
# an exact count in one request instead of a two-hour walk. `search=` names the
# agents in a category in about a second, which is what fills the four category
# pages with real BSC agents. And /feedbacks becomes readable at all — every row
# embeds the agent it rates and the transaction that wrote it, so one walk gives
# both the registry-wide reputation picture and per-agent evidence a user can
# check. That is the half of "is this agent real" that ownerOf cannot answer.
#
# The catch, and the reason `proven_count` exists: a filter this API does not
# implement is accepted and IGNORED, answering with the whole population under
# the label you asked for. So no share is published until the filter has been
# shown to apply.
#
# Blank is fine. It degrades to the narrower reading, and every payload carries
# a `tier` field saying which one answered.
#
# Note the name. This was first issued as `API_KEY_8004_pro`, and a lowercase
# suffix is invisible to tests/test_env_template.py — the guard that fails when
# this file and the code disagree matches ^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]{2,}$. A config name
# outside the guard's reach is one rename away from silently reverting the
# artifact to the anonymous tier, so it was renamed to be seen.
SCAN8004_API_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Observability. All three are optional and all three are now read.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the Warden loop serves its own /metrics. Unset — the default — starts no
# server, which is the right default for an agent: `make api` already exposes
# /metrics for the whole process, and an agent that opened a port nobody asked
# for would be a second listener to secure. Set it when the loop runs without the
# API beside it, which is the case where its counters are otherwise unreachable.
#
# Needs the extra: `uv sync --extra ops`. Without it every metric is a no-op and
# /metrics answers 501 naming the install rather than serving an empty body — an
# empty exposition and an idle agent are indistinguishable to a scraper.
PROMETHEUS_PORT=

# Telegram alerting. **Both or neither**: a token with no chat ids sends nowhere,
# and `alerts.configured()` reports that as unconfigured rather than letting a
# silent notifier look like a working one.
#
# Unset is silence, and silence is the design — `notify()` returns False and
# never raises, because a notifier that raises takes the agent down to announce
# that the agent is up. Nothing is queued for later; the journal is the durable
# record and the kill file is what the operator actually relies on.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_CHAT_IDS=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOT WIRED TO ANYTHING — listed so their absence is not mistaken for an omission
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ALTANA_API_KEY / ALTANA_SESSION_CONFIG — neither is needed. This said
#                                          "sessions/ is a docstring", which
#                                          stopped being true and stayed here;
#                                          `sessions/` now grants and revokes on
#                                          chapel through MISQUOTE_PRIVATE_KEY,
#                                          against addresses read from the SDK.
#                                          Altana's hosted relay would need a
#                                          key; sending the calls ourselves does
#                                          not, which is why there is none. P-27.
# WALLET_KEYSTORE_PATH                   — keystore unlocking is not implemented;
#                                          the signer reads MISQUOTE_PRIVATE_KEY
